---
// src/pages/blog/botox-vs-dysport-vs-xeomin.astro — comparison article; AI-Overview / PAA target.
// Links to /services/botox/. ⚕️ Medical director MUST review neuromodulator claims. Replace pricing.
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro"; // TODO: your real layout
import SchemaFAQ from "../../components/seo/SchemaFAQ.astro";
import SchemaBreadcrumb from "../../components/seo/SchemaBreadcrumb.astro";
import { business as b } from "../../config/business";

const title = "Botox vs Dysport vs Xeomin: Which Is Right for You? | House of Rose Aesthetics";
const description =
  "Botox vs Dysport vs Xeomin — how these three wrinkle relaxers compare on onset, spread, longevity and cost, and how to choose, from House of Rose Aesthetics in Punta Gorda, FL.";
const datePublished = "2026-08-01";
const url = Astro.url.href;

const faqItems = [
  { q: "What is the difference between Botox, Dysport and Xeomin?", a: "All three are botulinum toxin type A injectables (neuromodulators) that relax the muscles causing expression lines. They differ mainly in formulation: Botox is the original and most recognized; Dysport has smaller molecules that can take effect a little faster and spread across broader areas like the forehead; Xeomin is a 'naked' toxin with no added proteins, which some believe lowers the chance of building resistance over time." },
  { q: "Which one lasts the longest?", a: "For most people all three last about three to four months, and real-world longevity is similar. Individual factors — metabolism, dose, muscle strength and treatment area — affect duration more than the brand does." },
  { q: "Which works the fastest?", a: "Dysport often shows results a bit sooner, sometimes within two to three days, while Botox and Xeomin typically take three to five days, with full effect around two weeks for all three." },
  { q: "Is one better for certain areas?", a: "Dysport's spread makes it a nice choice for larger areas like the forehead, while Botox and Xeomin offer precise control for smaller, detailed areas such as around the eyes or lips. Your injector will recommend based on your anatomy and goals." },
  { q: "Do they cost the same?", a: "Pricing is usually per unit and comparable, though Dysport is dosed differently (more units for an equivalent effect), so the per-unit price looks lower while the total is similar. At House of Rose we quote by treatment area so your cost is clear up front." },
  { q: "How do I choose between them?", a: "The best choice depends on your goals, your anatomy and how your body has responded to treatments in the past — not on the brand name alone. We'll recommend the right product at your consultation, and we can adjust over time." },
];

const article = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "BlogPosting",
  headline: "Botox vs Dysport vs Xeomin: Which Is Right for You?",
  description, datePublished, dateModified: datePublished, mainEntityOfPage: url,
  author: { "@type": "Organization", name: b.name, url: b.url }, publisher: { "@id": `${b.url}/#business` },
};
---
<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(article)} />
  <SchemaFAQ items={faqItems} />
  <SchemaBreadcrumb items={[{ name: "Blog", url: "/blog/" }, { name: "Botox vs Dysport vs Xeomin", url: "/blog/botox-vs-dysport-vs-xeomin/" }]} />

  <article class="post">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Injectables · Guide</p>
      <h1>Botox vs Dysport vs Xeomin: Which Is Right for You?</h1>
      <p class="subhead">Three excellent wrinkle relaxers, three small but meaningful differences. Here's how to tell them apart — and how we choose for each client.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="quick-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>All three are FDA-approved botulinum toxin injectables that smooth expression lines, and all work well. <strong>Botox</strong> is the original, precise and predictable. <strong>Dysport</strong> tends to kick in a little faster and spreads nicely across larger areas like the forehead. <strong>Xeomin</strong> is a highly purified "naked" formula with no added proteins. Longevity is similar (~3–4 months). The best pick depends on your anatomy and goals, not the brand.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>At a glance</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Botox</th><th>Dysport</th><th>Xeomin</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>Onset</th><td>3–5 days</td><td>2–3 days (often fastest)</td><td>3–5 days</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Spread</th><td>Precise, localized</td><td>Spreads more — good for broad areas</td><td>Precise, localized</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Formulation</th><td>Original, with proteins</td><td>Smaller molecules</td><td>"Naked" — no added proteins</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Longevity</th><td>~3–4 months</td><td>~3–4 months</td><td>~3–4 months</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Great for</th><td>All areas, detailed work</td><td>Forehead &amp; larger areas</td><td>Sensitive patients, precision</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>So which should you get?</h2>
      <p>Honestly, for most people any of the three will give a beautiful, natural result — the artistry of your injector matters far more than the brand. We often suggest <strong>Dysport</strong> for broad forehead smoothing, <strong>Xeomin</strong> for clients who prefer a pure formula or have used toxins for years, and <strong>Botox</strong> as the reliable all-rounder. If one hasn't given you the result or longevity you wanted, switching can be worth trying.</p>
      <p><a href="/services/botox/">See our Botox &amp; injectables page →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-block">
      <h2>Let's find your best fit</h2>
      <p>House of Rose Aesthetics offers Botox, Dysport and Xeomin across Punta Gorda, Port Charlotte, Fort Myers and Cape Coral. Book a complimentary consultation for a natural, personalized recommendation.</p>
      <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Book your consultation</a>
    </section>

    <section class="faq">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      {faqItems.map((item) => (<details><summary>{item.q}</summary><p>{item.a}</p></details>))}
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .post { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 1.25rem 4rem; line-height: 1.65; }
  .eyebrow { text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .16em; font-size: .78rem; font-weight: 600; opacity: .7; margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5vw, 2.75rem); line-height: 1.1; margin: 0 0 .8rem; text-wrap: balance; }
  .subhead { font-size: 1.1rem; opacity: .85; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  section { margin-bottom: 2.25rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 .6rem; }
  .quick-answer { background: rgba(162,59,87,.07); border-left: 4px solid #a23b57; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; }
  .quick-answer h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; min-width: 560px; font-size: .9rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: .55rem .7rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.1); vertical-align: top; }
  tbody th { white-space: nowrap; }
  a { color: #a23b57; }
  .cta-block { background: rgba(0,0,0,.03); border-radius: 12px; padding: 1.5rem; text-align: center; }
  .cta { display: inline-block; padding: .8rem 1.4rem; border-radius: 999px; background: #a23b57; color: #fff; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; margin-top: .5rem; }
  .cta:hover { background: #7d2b41; }
  .faq details { border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.1); padding: .9rem 0; }
  .faq summary { font-weight: 600; cursor: pointer; font-size: 1.02rem; }
  .faq details p { margin: .7rem 0 0; opacity: .85; }
</style>
